  "grammar": {
    "title": "The dirham / rial trap",
    "body": "Prices are officially in **dirham** (درهم), but Moroccans — especially in souks and older folks — often quote in **rial**. The conversion: **1 dirham = 20 rial**. So '*meya*' (100) can mean 100 rial = **5 dirham**. If a price sounds wildly high, they're probably speaking rial — divide by 20. When in doubt: *b'dirham 3afak?* ('in dirham, please?').\nTo ask a price: **shhal?** (how much) or **shhal hada?** (how much is this)."
  },
  "canDo": [
    "Count to ten and recognize bigger prices (20, 50, 100)",
    "Ask how much something costs",
    "Handle the dirham-vs-rial confusion",
    "Start a friendly bargain"
  ],
